WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.500
We got a new backdrop!

00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:02.820
Thanks Flame Challenge!

00:00:05.740 --> 00:00:06.500
Hey Crazies.

00:00:06.500 --> 00:00:09.460
I’ve been thinking, there’s an idea we
haven’t given enough thought.

00:00:09.460 --> 00:00:10.880
Inertia.

00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:12.740
We defined it in a previous video.

00:00:12.740 --> 00:00:15.140
An object’s ability to resist changes in motion.

00:00:15.140 --> 00:00:17.060
But then Question Clone got involved.

00:00:17.060 --> 00:00:18.240
What does that really mean?

00:00:18.250 --> 00:00:20.680
And, well, I just can’t help but answer
the guy’s questions.

00:00:20.680 --> 00:00:23.520
First, let’s get some perspective with a
little history.

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:29.980
Even though real science started with Galileo,
the story of motion starts much much earlier.

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:34.700
Back when exact dates are a little hazy,
there was this guy named Aristotle

00:00:34.700 --> 00:00:38.380
in Ancient Greece.
and he had some very bad ideas.

00:00:38.380 --> 00:00:40.480
Introducing Aristotle Clone.

00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:43.480
The natural state of all things is to be at
rest.

00:00:43.480 --> 00:00:47.640
If you impart motion to a book, does it not
stop of its own accord?

00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:49.020
Get! Get out! Shoe!

00:00:49.020 --> 00:00:51.360
How dare you! Do you know who I am?!

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:53.280
You’re just a clone of Aristotle.

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:57.280
Anyway, I know I’ve said this before but
I’ll repeat it as many times as it takes.

00:00:57.280 --> 00:01:00.700
Aristotle was almost always wrong.

00:01:00.700 --> 00:01:05.299
But his ideas sounded pretty believable,
so humans were convinced he was right for

00:01:05.299 --> 00:01:07.689
about 2000 years.

00:01:07.689 --> 00:01:09.220
Fast forward to Galileo.

00:01:09.220 --> 00:01:13.259
Real science began when Galileo started doing
experiments.

00:01:13.259 --> 00:01:16.880
You know, actually testing our ideas before
claiming them as fact.

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:20.640
Galileo discovered something very important:
Relativity.

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:24.639
No no no, not the Einsteinian relativity,
just basic relativity.

00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:28.480
We did a whole video on it as part of a series,
but here’s the too long don’t watch version:

00:01:28.480 --> 00:01:34.940
Steady motion in a straight line for one observer
could easily be no motion for another observer.

00:01:34.940 --> 00:01:38.960
Therefore, they are the same type of motion: Inertial Motion.

00:01:38.960 --> 00:01:43.800
Inertial, as in Inertia, as in Inert-sha. Eh?

00:01:43.800 --> 00:01:45.299
Root words for the win!

00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:47.700
The point is, when Aristotle said this:

00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:51.039
The natural state of all things is to be at rest.

00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:53.840
He should have also included steady motion
in a straight line,

00:01:53.840 --> 00:01:56.620
because that’s indistinguishable from being
at rest.

00:01:56.620 --> 00:01:59.680
And this brings us to Newton and the Principia
Mathematica.

00:01:59.680 --> 00:02:01.860
I have a copy of this somewhere.

00:02:01.860 --> 00:02:03.100
Ah! Here it is.

00:02:04.760 --> 00:02:06.120
Let's read, shall we?

00:02:06.940 --> 00:02:13.320
Law 1: Every body perseveres in its state
of being at rest or of moving uniformly straight forward.

00:02:14.920 --> 00:02:18.080
Newton’s first law of motion actually defines
inertia.

00:02:18.840 --> 00:02:23.280
It’s a tendency things have to stay still
or keep steadily moving

00:02:23.280 --> 00:02:24.320
like this,

00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:27.300
or like this,

00:02:28.240 --> 00:02:29.380
or even this,

00:02:31.280 --> 00:02:33.140
If you want to know what that car video is all about,

00:02:33.140 --> 00:02:35.000
go check it out on my friend Jesse’s channel.

00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:36.760
He works out the math behind it too.

00:02:36.760 --> 00:02:38.440
But didn’t that car just stop?

00:02:38.440 --> 00:02:41.860
Yeah, because there’s this pesky thing on
Earth called friction.

00:02:41.860 --> 00:02:44.360
Without the friction, it would have just gone on forever.

00:02:44.370 --> 00:02:47.890
The point I’m trying to make here is that inertia
is not a force.

00:02:47.890 --> 00:02:51.660
I repeat, inertia is not a force!

00:02:51.660 --> 00:02:55.620
When you take all the forces away, inertia
is what remains.

00:02:55.620 --> 00:02:57.560
Thanks for liking and sharing this video.

00:02:57.560 --> 00:02:59.420
Please subscribe if you want to keep up with us.

00:02:59.420 --> 00:03:02.640
And until next time, remember, it’s OK to
be a little crazy.

00:03:04.640 --> 00:03:07.140
In the last video, we discussed where size
comes from.

00:03:07.140 --> 00:03:08.360
Comment response time!

00:03:08.360 --> 00:03:11.280
Mr Coffis isn’t sure what “space” is
anymore.

00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:13.840
Yeah, that’s actually a place we’re pretty
stuck right now.

00:03:13.840 --> 00:03:17.820
In quantum physics, it’s just an environment in which
things play out.

00:03:17.820 --> 00:03:20.260
It doesn’t really affect any of the interactions.

00:03:20.260 --> 00:03:24.580
In general relativity, it’s something tangible
that can be stretched and twisted.

00:03:24.580 --> 00:03:28.499
But it still has to be a continuous "fabric," otherwise
the equations aren’t valid.

00:03:28.499 --> 00:03:33.090
Either way though, it’s kind of just there
and isn’t made of anything more fundamental.

00:03:33.090 --> 00:03:36.240
Joel Craig always assumed space was energy
fields.

00:03:36.240 --> 00:03:40.120
Well, like I said in the previous answer,
space is just kind of there.

00:03:40.120 --> 00:03:43.580
In fact, it has to already be there for our
models to work.

00:03:43.580 --> 00:03:47.380
Even in Quantum Field Theory, there’s an
energy field for every type of fundamental

00:03:47.380 --> 00:03:50.740
particle, but those fields have to exist in
a space.

00:03:50.740 --> 00:03:52.140
That’s what a “field” is.

00:03:52.140 --> 00:03:54.880
It’s a value assigned to every point in
space.

00:03:54.880 --> 00:03:56.920
No space means no field.

00:03:56.920 --> 00:04:01.220
Pablo Perez was wondering if a calculus differential
was the same as a single point.

00:04:01.220 --> 00:04:02.160
Definitely not.

00:04:02.160 --> 00:04:04.360
It’s always the space between two points.

00:04:04.360 --> 00:04:07.360
Say you’ve got a point at 5 and a
point at 7.

00:04:07.360 --> 00:04:13.580
The difference is 2, so that’s like saying
the points are at 5 and 5+2.

00:04:13.580 --> 00:04:14.620
Everybody still with me?

00:04:14.620 --> 00:04:19.080
You could make the points closer by moving
the second point to 5+1,

00:04:19.080 --> 00:04:23.280
or 5+0.1, or 5+0.000001.

00:04:23.280 --> 00:04:27.080
If you make the difference so small that you
can’t hope to measure it,

00:04:27.080 --> 00:04:30.200
then the difference is called a “differential.”

00:04:30.200 --> 00:04:35.360
We say that one is at 5 and 5+dx,
that little tiny space,

00:04:35.360 --> 00:04:37.320
but those are still two different points.

00:04:37.320 --> 00:04:38.660
Same goes for volumes.

00:04:38.660 --> 00:04:41.420
Little volume increments are made of 8 different
points

00:04:41.420 --> 00:04:44.340
and, depending on your choice of coordinates,
can have different shapes.

00:04:44.340 --> 00:04:47.040
But that’s because differentials always
have some size.

00:04:47.040 --> 00:04:50.580
Diodoubled wants to know what String Theory
has to say about this.

00:04:50.580 --> 00:04:53.820
They definitely go with solution #3:
points have size.

00:04:53.820 --> 00:04:57.040
Which reminds me,
I asked you which of the three solutions you

00:04:57.040 --> 00:04:58.200
thought was correct.

00:04:58.200 --> 00:05:01.500
Infinities, unoccupiable spots, or points
with size.

00:05:01.500 --> 00:05:04.800
“Points with size” was the winner by consensus,
although not by much.

00:05:04.800 --> 00:05:07.260
Because very few of you actually voted in
the comments.

00:05:07.260 --> 00:05:09.270
Come on crazies! Work with me here!

00:05:09.270 --> 00:05:11.700
We did have some very witty people in the
comments this time.

00:05:11.700 --> 00:05:12.940
Much appreciated.

00:05:12.940 --> 00:05:15.840
Anyway, thanks for watching, everybody! See you in the comments.

